// =============================================================================
// des_generic.h — problem-agnostic distributed homomorphic DES supervisor
// =============================================================================
//
// Runs ANY supervisor set generated by the UltraDES notebook, partitioned over
// ANY number of nodes, over ANY transport implementing DesTransport. Nothing in
// this file is specific to a plant, to a board, or to a protocol.
//
// Everything is derived at boot from the generated header:
//   • how many supervisors there are, how big they are, what they constrain;
//   • which supervisor goes on which node;
//   • which events are LOCAL to a node (no traffic) and which are SHARED;
//   • who initiates each event and who must participate in it;
//   • which events are controllable (see DES_CONTROLLABLE_MASK — this is the
//     one property the generated header does not carry).
//
// Verified against every header in this repo:
//   small_factory           4 events,  1 supervisor
//   extended_small_factory  6 events,  2 supervisors
//   fms                    31 events,  7 supervisors (up to 164 states)
//
// ── PORTABILITY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Depends only on mbedTLS, the C++ standard library and a DesTransport. It does
// NOT depend on Arduino, FreeRTOS or ESP-IDF: the four platform touchpoints
// (logging, milliseconds, microseconds, yield) are macros at the top, defaulting
// to Arduino and falling back to POSIX. The same file builds for a Linux
// gateway, an industrial PC or another MCU family.
//
// ── PROTOCOL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Conjunctive fusion over the nodes that constrain an event, with the
// controllable/uncontrollable distinction driving two different mechanisms:
//
//   local event            apply locally                       0 frames
//   shared + controllable  REQ → VOTE* → COMMIT → ACK*         2+2p frames
//   shared + uncontrollab. NOTIFY → ACK*                       1+p  frames
//                                                   (p = number of participants)
//
// An uncontrollable event cannot be vetoed — a supervisor may not disable it,
// and by the time we hear of it the plant has already done it. Asking
// permission would be meaningless; it gets a reliable notification instead.
//
// Concurrency: participants hold a lock between VOTE-YES and COMMIT/ABORT, so
// two initiators can never interleave transactions on the same supervisor. A
// YES is never withdrawn for another request: a locked participant refuses all.
// An initiator still collecting votes yields to an older (lower-numbered)
// requester: WOUND-WAIT (Rosenkrantz et al. 1978). Every request is answered
// at once, so no cycle of waits can form; a refused initiator has applied
// nothing, and retries with backoff.
//
// Loss is handled by retransmission until every participant acknowledges; the
// per-sender sequence number only rejects duplicates. A peer that reboots is
// recognised by its per-boot epoch, and boards built with different settings
// by a configuration fingerprint in every frame. When the budget is exhausted,
// or a peer restarts after shared events, a node SAFE-HALTS rather than
// continue on state that may have diverged. That is the Two Generals trade,
// made explicit: safety preserved, liveness sacrificed.
//
// Plant check: a synthesised supervisor never disables an uncontrollable event
// the plant can produce, so a reported uncontrollable event that ours disable is
// physically impossible — a sensor fault or a forged report — and is rejected
// rather than applied. Only supervisors synthesised over the plant (full local
// modular, monolithic) catch every such event; a reduced one may allow it.
// =============================================================================

#pragma once

#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
